2009_level2_solutions.pdf,14,b,"How many zeros are there at the end of the product of the first 20 primes?
(a) 0
(b) 1
(c) 2
(d) 5
(e) None of the above",": If there were 2 or more zeros, the product would be divisible by 100. But 100 = 2\cdot2\cdot5\cdot5,
and since in the product of the first 20 primes each prime number appears only once, there has to be
less than two zeros. On the other hand, 2 and 5 are both among the first 20 primes, and 2 \cdot 5 = 10, so
the product of the first 20 primes will be divisible by 10 and will have at least 1 zero at the end.",204aaa
