2010_level1_solutions.pdf,12,a,"Two buses leave the terminal at 8 A.M. Bus number 36 takes 85 minutes to complete its route;
bus number 86 takes 102 minutes. When is the next time the two buses will arrive together
at the terminal (assuming they are on time and they spend no time waiting between routes)?
(a) in 510 min
(b) in 102 min
(c) in 289 min
(d) in 408 min
(e) in 8,670 min",": The number of minutes before they both arrive together at the terminal must
be the least common multiple of 85 and 102.
85 = 5 \cdot 17
102 = 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 17
The least common multiple of the two numbers is then 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdot 17 = 10 \cdot 51 = 510.",226aaa
2010_level1_solutions.pdf,13,d,"Let a = 25 \cdot 53 \cdot 112. Let b be such that the greatest common factor of a and b is 2 \cdot 53 \cdot 11
and the least common multiple of a and b is 25 \cdot 33 \cdot 53 \cdot 112. Find b.
(a) b = 2 \cdot 33 \cdot 52 \cdot 112
(b) b = 25 \cdot 33 \cdot 53 \cdot 112
(c) b = 25 \cdot 35 \cdot 56 \cdot 11
(d) b = 2 \cdot 33 \cdot 53 \cdot 11
(e) b = 2 \cdot 3 \cdot 5 \cdot 11",": Since 2 \cdot 53 \cdot 11 is the greatest common factor of a and b, b must be equal
to 2 \cdot 53 \cdot 11 \cdot c where c is not divisible by 2, 5 and 11. On the other hand, c must be a
factor of the least common multiple. The only factor of the least common multiple that is
not divisible by 2, 5 or 11 is 33. Therefore b must be 3k for some k \le3. Suppose k < 3. Then
25 \cdot 3k \cdot 53 \cdot 112 would be a common multiple of a and b, however, since it is less than the
given least common multiple of a and b, this is a contradiction. Therefore k = 3, c = 33 and
b = 2 \cdot 33 \cdot 53 \cdot 11.
Another solution:
For any two positive integers a and b, a \cdot b = LCM(a, b) \cdot GCF(a, b).
Therefore
a
= 25 \cdot 33 \cdot 53 \cdot 112 \cdot 2 \cdot 53 \cdot 11
b = LCM(a, b) \cdot GCD(a, b)
25 \cdot 53 \cdot 112
= 2 \cdot 33 \cdot 53 \cdot 11.",227aaa
