2011_M_Olymp_Level_1_Sol.pdf,10,E,"How many zeros are at the end of 17!? (Recall that n! is the product of the
first n positive integers, that is 2! = 2 \cdot 1, 3! = 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1, and so on)
(a) 2
(b) 3
(c) 17
(d) 21
(e) none of the above
The answer is: (b)","ach zero at the end of n! represents one time that 10 divides n!. So
the question to answer is how many times does 10 divide 17!? Since 10 = 5 \cdot 2 we
need to count how many times 5 divides 17! and then how many times 2 divides 17!.
Looking at the number of factors in 17! that are divisible by 5 we find 3, namely
5, 10, and 15. There are certainly more than 3 factors of 2 in 17! as the factor 16
alone has 4 two's. So there will be exactly 3 zeroes at the end of 17!.",275aaa
2011_M_Olymp_Level_1_Sol.pdf,11,A,"A train moving 45 miles per hour meets and is passed by a train moving 35
miles per hour in a opposite direction. A passenger in the first train sees the second
train takes 6 seconds to pass him. How long is the second train?
(a) 701 feet
(b) 698 feet
(c) 704 feet
(d) 710 feet
(e) none of the above
The answer is: (c)","s the two trains are moving at the same time the speed of the other
train should appear to the passenger as 45 mph +35 mph. Now using convertion of
1 mile = 5280 feet and 1 hour=3600 seconds, we have
(45 + 35) \cdot 5280 \cdot 6
3600
= 704 feet.",276aaa
