2012_MO_L2Sol.pdf,5,,"Suppose a group of people have a code between themselves on how they can send messages
to others in the group.
{\textbullet} For each subgroup of distinct people A, B and C in the group the following holds: if A
can send a message to B and B can send a message to C, then C can send a message to A.
{\textbullet} For each pair of distinct people A and B in the group, either A can send a message to
B or B can send a message to A but not both.
2

SVSU 2012 Math Olympics","Level II-page 3 of 13
What is the largest number of people in the group?
(a) 1
(b) 2
(c) 3
(d) 4
(e) none of the above
The answer is: (c)
Solution: Let X be the group of people. Sending of messages among the group translates
to a relation R on X (a subset of X \times X) such that if (A, B) \inR and (B, C) \inR, then
(C, A) \inR and either (A, B) \inR or (B, A) \inR but not both.
A relation with these
properties forces X to have at most 3 elements, since any relation with 4 or more elements
