Since x1 and x2 are integers, 1 + 4n = m2 for some positive integer m. Since 1 + 4n
is odd, m must be odd, i.e., m = 2k + 1 for some integer k. Every odd square is of
the form 1 + 4n since
m2 = (2k + 1)2 = 4k2 + 4k + 1 = 4(k2 + k) + 1.
Since 1 \len \le100, the answer is the number of odd squares between 5 = 1+4\cdot1 and
401 = 1+4\cdot100. There are nine odd squares in that range, 32, 52, 72, 92, 112, 132, 152, 172, 192.",379aaa
