2014_MO_L2Sol.pdf,2,,"Let f(x, y) be defined by f(x, 0) = x and f(x, y+1) = f(f(x, y), y): which of the following
is the largest?
(a) f(10, 15)
(b) f(11, 14)
(c)f(12, 13)
(d) f(13, 12)
(e) f(14, 11)
The answer is: (e)","Remark that f(x, 1) = f(f(x, 0), 0) = f(x, 0) = x, f(x, 2) = f(f(x, 1), 1) =
f(x, 1) = x, . . . , f(x, n) = x. So the biggest value is f(14, 11), since it has the largest x
value.",440aaa
2014_MO_L2Sol.pdf,3,,"How many integers can be expressed as a sum of three distinct numbers chosen from the
set {4, 7, 10, 13, . . . , 46}?
(a) 36
(b) 37
(c) 42
(d) 43
(e) 45
The answer is: (b)","Since each number is of the form 1 + 3n, n = 1, 2, 3, . . . , 15, the sum of the three
numbers will be of the form 3+3k+3l+3m where k, l and m are chosen from {1, 2, 3, . . . , 15}.
So the question is equivalent to the easier question of, ""How many distinct integers can be
formed by adding three numbers from, {1, 2, 3, . . . , 15}?""
The smallest is 1+ 2 + 3 = 6 and the largest is 13 +14 +15 = 42. It is clearly possible to
get every sum between 6 and 42 by:
- increasing the sum by one replacing a number with one that is 1 larger or,
1

SVSU 2014 Math Olympics
Solution Level II-page 2 of 12
- decreasing the sum by one by decreasing one of the addends by 1.
Thus all the integers from 6 to 42 inclusive can be formed. The answer, of course, is 37.",441aaa
2014_MO_L2Sol.pdf,4,,"How many solutions does the equation
sin(x) sin(2x) sin(3x) \cdot \cdot \cdot sin(11x) sin(12x) = 0
have in the interval (0, \pi]?
(a) 12
(b) 24
(c) 46
(d) 68
(e) none of the above
The answer is: (c)","If x is a solution of the equation, then lx = k\pi for some integers k and l with
1 \lel \le12. Thus x = k
l \pi. Since we want x to be in (0, \pi], the number of solutions equals the
number of fractions in (0, 1] with numerator an integer, and denominator a positive integer
\le12. Since we want to avoid counting the same solution twice (for example 2\pi/3 = 4\pi/6),
we need to count only fractions in lowest terms. Let \varphi(n) be the number of fractions in
lowest terms in (0, 1] with denominator n. Then \varphi(1) = 1, \varphi(2) = 1, \varphi(3) = 2, \varphi(4) = 2,
\varphi(5) = 4, \varphi(6) = 2, \varphi(7) = 6, \varphi(8) = 4, \varphi(9) = 6, \varphi(10) = 4, \varphi(11) = 10, \varphi(12) = 4. Adding
the above values of \varphi we get that the number of solutions is 46. (Note that \varphi(n) represents
the number of positive integers \len and relatively prime to n. In elementary number theory
class \varphi(n) is the so called Euler function.)",442aaa
