Level I Solutions --- page 4 of 11",": Let the radius of the smaller circle be r cm and let the radius of the larger circle
be R cm. Thus, the circumference of the smaller circle is 2\pir cm, the circumference of the
larger circle is 2\piR cm, the area of the smaller circle is \pir 2 cm2, and the area of the larger
circle is \piR2 cm2. Since the sum of the radii of the two circles is 10 cm, then r +R = 10. Since
the circumference of the larger circle is 3 cm larger than the circumference of the smaller
