2017_level1_solutions.pdf,18,a,"Abby, Benny, and Louie together have 100 pennies. If Abby had six times as many as she
has now and Benny had one third as many as he has now, there would still be 100 pennies.
Louie says, ``It's not fair, I have fewer than 30.'' How many pennies does Louie have?
(a) 15
(b) 25
(c) 28
(d) 29
(e) None of the above",": If A is the number of pennies Abby has and B is the number of pennies Benny
has, then A and B must satisfy A + B = 6A + 1/3B and 70 < A + B < 100. If we multiply
both sides of the equation by 3 and simplify we get 2B = 15A. Since A and B are integers,
and 2 and 15 don't share any factors, we must have A = 2k and B = 15k for some integer k.
So A + B = 17k. Since A + B has to be between 70 and 100, k has to be 5. So A + B = 85, and
Louie has 100 -85 = 15 pennies.

2017 SVSU Math Olympics
Level I Solutions --- page 8 of 11",554aaa
